WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:05.700
Dead bodies under a bar. Back-stabbing friends.&nbsp;
Hooking up with the boss. Goodfellas may have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.700 --> 00:00:10.620
forever glamorized life in the mob, but the&nbsp;
real events it's based on are so much darker.

00:00:11.201 --> 00:00:13.020
In Goodfellas, Henry Hill goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.020 --> 00:00:16.200
from life as a kid on the street&nbsp;
to a full-fledged gangster.

00:00:16.378 --> 00:00:19.798
"As far back as I could remember&nbsp;
I always wanted to be a gangster."

00:00:19.798 --> 00:00:24.240
In real life, Hill took a little break from life&nbsp;
on the streets and briefly pursued a career in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:28.290
the U.S. Military. According to military&nbsp;
community publication We Are the Mighty,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.290 --> 00:00:33.060
Hill joined the 82nd Airborne at age 17.&nbsp;
The article mentions how Hill had joined&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.840
the military as a way to escape a senate&nbsp;
probe into union connections to organized&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:36.840 --> 00:00:41.280
crime. It also mentions how he used his time&nbsp;
there to continue his criminal activities.

00:00:41.280 --> 00:00:44.760
According to the We Are the Mighty&nbsp;
article, Hill was a loan shark in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:44.760 --> 00:00:49.110
military and got into plenty of fights while&nbsp;
serving. When his enlistment time was up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:49.110 --> 00:00:53.580
Hill returned to his life of crime, becoming&nbsp;
a different kind of soldier for New York's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.580 --> 00:00:57.000
Lucchese [loo-KAY-zee] crime family. Having&nbsp;
briefly served the government that eventually&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.000 --> 00:01:00.718
brought him down, it seems that Henry&nbsp;
Hill was a study in contradictions.

00:01:01.401 --> 00:01:03.360
Henry Hill was involved in a lot&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.360 --> 00:01:07.620
of schemes with the Lucchese family. Ironically,&nbsp;
one of the biggest ones, and the one which really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:07.620 --> 00:01:13.140
brought him and some of his closest associates&nbsp;
down, was a point-shaving scheme with the 1978–79&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.140 --> 00:01:17.760
Boston College basketball team. This is one&nbsp;
of the stories that transcends Goodfellas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:17.760 --> 00:01:22.980
as some have called it the worst point-fixing&nbsp;
scheme in sports history. Not only because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.980 --> 00:01:26.760
was so easily found out, but because it&nbsp;
worked out so poorly for the gangsters.

00:01:26.760 --> 00:01:30.480
Stories differ across the board.&nbsp;
Some players say the scheme never&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:32.820
took place beyond some preliminary conversations,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:37.500
while some gangsters say they had multiple&nbsp;
players in their pockets. In terms of the payout,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.500 --> 00:01:41.640
a few gangsters claimed to have raked in&nbsp;
hundreds of thousands. Others, meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:41.640 --> 00:01:46.200
say they didn't score more than a few thousand.&nbsp;
Nevertheless, the scheme accomplished one goal:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:51.240
It brought down Jimmy Burke, also known as Jimmy&nbsp;
Conway in Goodfellas. Henry Hill's testimony about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:56.700
Burke's involvement in this half-baked scheme is&nbsp;
what finally got Jimmy "The Gent" behind bars.

00:01:57.334 --> 00:01:59.220
Goodfellas ends with Henry Hill in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:59.220 --> 00:02:01.528
witness protection living a life in the suburbs.

00:02:01.528 --> 00:02:03.900
"I get to live the rest of&nbsp;
my life like a schnook."

00:02:03.900 --> 00:02:07.680
Luckily for Hill, though, he wouldn't live&nbsp;
out the remainder of his life as an average&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:07.680 --> 00:02:11.880
suburbanite under a false identity. He also&nbsp;
wasn't being actively hunted by the mafia,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.880 --> 00:02:14.340
either. As noted in his New York Times obituary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:14.340 --> 00:02:18.900
Hill was kicked out of hiding due to some&nbsp;
questionable moves. Hill's obituary noted:

00:02:25.260 --> 00:02:28.800
Henry Hill also revealed his identity&nbsp;
often while in witness protection,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:28.800 --> 00:02:33.840
meaning he basically snitched on himself —&nbsp;
just to a different set of people. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.840 --> 00:02:38.940
Hill was never killed by the mafia — even after&nbsp;
going public about who he really was — as most of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.940 --> 00:02:42.720
the people he feared were already in prison.&nbsp;
He eventually died of health problems that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:42.720 --> 00:02:47.002
reportedly included heart disease and other&nbsp;
illnesses related to heavy cigarette use.

00:02:47.601 --> 00:02:50.640
In Goodfellas, Henry meets his&nbsp;
eventual wife Karen on a double&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.640 --> 00:02:53.760
date with Tommy DeVito.&nbsp;
It was just two friends with two girls,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:53.760 --> 00:02:58.920
and Hill even stands up Karen on the second date.&nbsp;
This timeline is somewhat accurate, except for the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:58.920 --> 00:03:02.400
fact that Henry Hill was actually invited on a&nbsp;
double date by Paul Vario's son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.400 --> 00:03:07.200
Paul Vario Jr. In the film, this would be Paul&nbsp;
Cicero's son, who is not seen in the movie.

00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:12.120
By going on the date, Hill wasn't just doing his&nbsp;
buddy a favor; he was helping out his boss's son.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:12.120 --> 00:03:16.500
Little Paulie was trying to go on a date with a&nbsp;
Jewish girl who refused to go on a date without&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:21.660
it being a double date. So, of course, who does&nbsp;
he call up but one of his fellow wiseguys. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:21.660 --> 00:03:25.200
rest of the early courtship plays out the&nbsp;
same in real life as it does in the movie.

00:03:25.689 --> 00:03:30.420
Paulie Cicero seems relatively "moral"&nbsp;
compared to the other madcap gangsters&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:30.420 --> 00:03:35.340
in Goodfellas. Yet he has one line that goes&nbsp;
beyond the script. While convincing Henry to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.340 --> 00:03:39.540
go back to his wife, who's upset over Hill's&nbsp;
many infidelities, Paul says to his friend:

00:03:39.540 --> 00:03:41.668
"I know how to talk to her, especially to her."

00:03:41.668 --> 00:03:47.220
The "especially to her" line seems strange&nbsp;
unless you take it to mean that Paulie has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:47.220 --> 00:03:51.120
special relationship with Karen. In Hill's&nbsp;
book Gangsters and Goodfellas: The Mob,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:51.120 --> 00:03:55.380
Witness Protection, and Life on the Run, he&nbsp;
writes that Karen had an affair with Paul Vario,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.380 --> 00:03:59.220
Cicero's real-life counterpart, while&nbsp;
Henry was in prison. It makes sense&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.220 --> 00:04:02.760
given how tough it was for Karen to&nbsp;
keep afloat while Henry was in jail.

00:04:03.501 --> 00:04:05.580
Joe Pesci's Tommy DeVito in Goodfellas&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:05.580 --> 00:04:10.440
was a perpetual bachelor with a violent intensity&nbsp;
that seemed to be grounded in an inferiority&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:14.880
complex. In real life, though, DeSimone&nbsp;
was a big man with a wife. He&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:14.880 --> 00:04:19.080
was also no less psychotic, though. In Goodfellas,&nbsp;
we see Tommy frighten his closest friends&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:19.080 --> 00:04:23.880
by pretending to be offended by being called&nbsp;
funny in the epic and tense "funny how" scene.

00:04:23.880 --> 00:04:26.737
"Funny how? I mean what's funny about it?"

00:04:26.737 --> 00:04:29.940
We also see him shoot and kill Spider, played&nbsp;
by Michael Imperioli, in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:33.840
the film, which happened in real life according to&nbsp;
accounts. In Nicholas Pileggi's book&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:33.840 --> 00:04:40.140
Wiseguy, on which the film was based, Henry Hill&nbsp;
also details how Tommy DeSimone, at just 17, was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.140 --> 00:04:44.520
walking with Hill when he decided to show off by&nbsp;
shooting and killing a random man in cold blood.

00:04:44.520 --> 00:04:48.900
Furthermore, DeSimone supposedly killed his&nbsp;
own best friend, according to some sources&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.900 --> 00:04:53.400
and the Pileggi book. He's also mentioned&nbsp;
across most outlets as being over 6 feet tall&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:53.400 --> 00:04:57.840
and a burly enforcer, despite being played&nbsp;
by the small-statured Pesci in the film.

00:04:58.440 --> 00:05:03.480
Jimmy Burke, also known as Jimmy Conway, was an&nbsp;
Irish gangster and a bit of a mentor to Henry&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.480 --> 00:05:08.040
Hill. It makes sense, then, that he would lead&nbsp;
his own son into the lucrative life of the mafia.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:08.040 --> 00:05:11.880
Burke reportedly included his son, Francis James&nbsp;
Burke, in the Lufthansa heist.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.880 --> 00:05:16.380
In Wiseguy, Hill recounts how proud Tommy&nbsp;
DeSimone was to take James on his first hit:

00:05:22.080 --> 00:05:26.400
Unfortunately, it didn't work out as well&nbsp;
for Frank as it had for his pops. James was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.400 --> 00:05:30.780
found shot dead at 26, while his father was&nbsp;
in jail. The police claimed the murder was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:30.780 --> 00:05:34.140
drug-related. One can only imagine the&nbsp;
reflection Jimmy "The Gent" was going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:34.140 --> 00:05:37.740
through after having seen his son and&nbsp;
DeSimone, who was almost like a son,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:37.740 --> 00:05:41.040
get whacked, while Burke himself was&nbsp;
planning on killing his own protege.

00:05:41.734 --> 00:05:46.740
In Wiseguy, Henry Hill explores a bit deeper&nbsp;
the fact that Paul Vario, and by extension&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.740 --> 00:05:50.580
everybody under Vario including him, was a&nbsp;
capo in the Lucchese crime family.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:50.580 --> 00:05:54.840
Most people with some knowledge have heard of&nbsp;
the "Five Families" and the Lucchese crew was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:59.160
one. The Gambino crew was another,&nbsp;
and around the time of the events in Goodfellas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.160 --> 00:06:03.120
the legendary John Gotti would&nbsp;
take control of the Gambinos. In the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:07.560
Hill even mentions that Billy Batts was a&nbsp;
Gambino family member, thus the reason Tommy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:12.360
DeVito has to pay for murdering him. Given all&nbsp;
the stories about feuds between mafia families,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:12.360 --> 00:06:17.220
one might wonder why Vario would kill one of&nbsp;
his up-and-coming soldiers. Around this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.220 --> 00:06:20.040
however, the Gambinos and the&nbsp;
Luccheses were very close.

00:06:20.040 --> 00:06:25.140
In 1962 Carlo Gambino's eldest son&nbsp;
married Tommy Lucchese's daughter in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:25.680 --> 00:06:28.020
1,000-person wedding that garnered quite a bit of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.020 --> 00:06:32.373
press. That means that the heads of two&nbsp;
of the families were literally family.

00:06:32.940 --> 00:06:37.320
Being a mob picture, most of the action&nbsp;
in Goodfellas happens in bars and lounges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:37.320 --> 00:06:42.120
and no location is more front and center than&nbsp;
Robert's Lounge. That's probably because Jimmy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.120 --> 00:06:46.380
Conway owned it. While he refers to it when he&nbsp;
tells Billy Batts drinks are "on the house,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:46.380 --> 00:06:50.580
the fact that Jimmy had a lounge and later a&nbsp;
jacket warehouse speaks to the "legitimate"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.580 --> 00:06:55.080
businesses he owned. Jimmy Conway didn't do&nbsp;
too bad for a guy who could never be made.

00:06:55.080 --> 00:07:00.900
In real life, Robert's Lounge was a legendary&nbsp;
mobster social club and yes, Jimmy Burke did own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:00.900 --> 00:07:05.940
it. When the bar was raided, bones were eventually&nbsp;
found in the basement. One set was said to have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.940 --> 00:07:11.040
belonged to Marty Krugman — the inspiration for&nbsp;
Morris Kessler, the wig king, who tips Jimmy off&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:11.040 --> 00:07:14.820
to the Lufthansa heist in the movie. The other&nbsp;
set of bones was said to have belonged to Tommy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:20.040
DeSimone. That revelation makes all of Jimmy's&nbsp;
sadness and anger over Tommy DeVito's death in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:24.360
the movie seem that much stranger given that&nbsp;
he helped the mafia hide DeSimone's body.

00:07:25.266 --> 00:07:26.760
It is a bit frightening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.760 --> 00:07:31.680
to discover that the real-life Tommy DeSimone&nbsp;
was more violent and uncontrollable than the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:31.680 --> 00:07:36.180
character played by Joe Pesci. In real life,&nbsp;
DeSimone was equal parts premeditated and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:36.180 --> 00:07:40.560
impulsive with little concern for human&nbsp;
life. He had no problem crossing lines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:40.560 --> 00:07:44.880
such as when he murdered Billy Batts. But&nbsp;
another line he crossed, according to Henry Hill,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.880 --> 00:07:50.038
was attempting to beat up and rape Hill's wife,&nbsp;
Karen. As Hill writes in Gangsters and Goodfellas,

00:07:54.838 --> 00:07:58.680
Hill goes on to claim he didn't find out&nbsp;
until a decade later and would have done&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:58.680 --> 00:08:02.640
something about it had he known. The irony&nbsp;
is that Karen's husband wasn't even the man&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:02.640 --> 00:08:06.900
DeSimone really had to worry about. Later&nbsp;
on in the book, Hill says that Paul Vario&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.900 --> 00:08:11.460
found out about this attempted rape, which is&nbsp;
what finally convinced him to kill DeSimone as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.460 --> 00:08:15.600
retribution for the Batts affair. Since Vario&nbsp;
was allegedly having an affair with Karen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:15.600 --> 00:08:20.760
DeSimone might as well have been signing his own&nbsp;
death warrant the moment he put his hands on her.

